export const EXTRA_BODIES: Record<string, string> = {
  'gross-vs-net-pay': `## The number you agreed to is not the number that arrives

An offer says $52,000 a year. Divided by 26 pay periods, that looks like $2,000 every two weeks. The first payslip says something closer to $1,500, and nothing has gone wrong.

The difference has a name. **Gross pay** is what you agreed to. **Net pay** is what lands. Everything between them was taken before you saw it.

## Where it goes

- **Federal income tax.** Withheld from every cheque based on the W-4 you filled in on day one.
- **FICA.** 7.65% of your pay, split between Social Security (6.2%) and Medicare (1.45%). Your employer quietly pays the same again on top.
- **State income tax**, unless you are in one of the nine states without one.
- **Your own deductions.** Health insurance premiums, and any 401(k) contribution.

> FICA is not optional and it is not a mistake. Every employed person in the country pays it on the first dollar they earn.

## Why the first one is often the worst

Withholding assumes your current cheque is what every cheque will look like for the whole year. Start a job in September and the system briefly behaves as though you have earned that rate since January, which can over-withhold. That money is not gone. It comes back as a refund.

## What to actually do

- **Read the payslip once, properly.** Find gross, find each deduction, find net. Ten minutes, once, and payslips stop being mysterious forever.
- **Check the W-4 if the withholding looks wrong.** You can update it any time, not just when hired.
- **Budget on NET, never gross.** This is the whole point. A budget built on $2,000 when $1,500 arrives fails in week one, and it fails in a way that feels like personal failure rather than an arithmetic error.

The gap is usually 20-30%. Knowing that before the first payslip is the difference between a surprise and a plan.`,

  'asking-for-more-money': `## The highest-paid ninety seconds of your life

A raise compounds. Ask successfully once at 24 and you do not earn that amount once. You earn it, and a percentage of it, every year after, on every future raise calculated from the new base.

Most people never ask. Not because they weigh it up and decide against, but because the conversation feels rude.

## It is not rude, and here is why

You are not asking a person for a favour out of their own pocket. You are asking an organisation to reprice a role, using information it already has. Managers have this conversation constantly. Yours is not the awkward one.

## Before you open your mouth

- **Find the range.** Job postings for the same title in your city, levels.fyi, Glassdoor, and anyone you can ask directly. You want a range, not a number.
- **Write down what you did.** Not duties, outcomes. "Cut the weekly close from three days to one" beats "responsible for reporting".
- **Decide your walk-away.** Not to use it. To stop yourself accepting something you will resent in four months.

## The actual conversation

> "Based on what I have taken on this year and what the market looks like for this role, I would like to talk about moving my salary to X."

Then stop talking.

The silence after a number is uncomfortable and it is doing work. People who fill it negotiate against themselves.

## Two rules that decide the outcome

- **Never say the first number if you can avoid it.** "What is the range budgeted for this role?" is a complete, professional answer to "what are you looking for?"
- **A range is a ceiling, not a midpoint.** Say "$70,000 to $75,000" and you will be offered $70,000. Ask for $75,000.

## When the answer is no

Ask what specifically would need to be true, and by when. A real answer gives you a plan. A vague answer tells you something too, just not the thing you asked about.`,

  'security-deposit': `## Most deposits are lost before you unpack

A security deposit is not a fee. It is your money, held against damage you might do, and it is legally supposed to come back. In practice a large share never does, and the reason is almost never that someone wrecked the place.

It is that nobody can prove what the place looked like when they arrived.

## The ten minutes that decide it

Before a single box comes in, photograph everything. Not a sweep of the room. Specific, dated, close.

- Every wall, every floor, every corner
- Inside the oven, the fridge, under the sink
- Every existing scuff, stain, crack and chip
- Window and door frames, especially any mould

Email the set to yourself and to the landlord the same day. The timestamp is the point. A photo in your camera roll is evidence; a photo the landlord acknowledged receiving is a much better one.

## Wear and tear is not damage

This is the line most disputes turn on, and the law is usually on your side.

- **Normal wear:** carpet flattened by walking, paint faded by sun, small nail holes, a worn door handle.
- **Damage:** a hole in the wall, a burn in the counter, a broken window, pet stains.

> A landlord may not charge you for the passage of time. Four years of ordinary living is not damage, and repainting between tenants is usually a cost of doing business.

## On the way out

- Give notice in **writing**, in the window the lease specifies.
- Clean properly. Most deductions are cleaning charges, and most cleaning charges are avoidable in an afternoon.
- Photograph everything **again**, empty.
- Ask to be present at the final inspection. Many places give you that right, and being in the room changes what gets written down.

## If it does not come back

Most states require the deposit returned within 14 to 30 days, with an itemised list of any deductions. No list usually means no valid deductions. Ask in writing, cite the deadline, and keep the reply.`,

  'rent-is-not-wasted': `## The sentence that costs people money

"Renting is throwing money away" is repeated so often it stops sounding like a claim. It is one, and comparing it honestly changes what a lot of people do at 25.

## Owning has money that vanishes too

The mortgage payment is not the cost of owning. Part of it is principal, which is genuinely yours. Everything else is gone the same way rent is.

- **Mortgage interest.** In the early years this is most of the payment. On a 30-year loan, year one is roughly 75-80% interest.
- **Property tax.** Every year, forever, whether or not the loan is paid off.
- **Insurance**, which is higher than renters insurance by an order of magnitude.
- **Maintenance.** Budget around 1% of the home's value a year. A roof is not optional and it does not build equity.
- **Closing costs**, 2-5% on the way in, and around 6% to an agent on the way out.

> The honest comparison is rent against interest + tax + insurance + maintenance. Not rent against the whole mortgage payment.

Run that and renting often wins for the first five to seven years, because the money that vanishes is similar and the transaction costs are enormous.

## What renting actually buys

- **Mobility.** Taking a job in another city costs you a month's notice, not 8% of a house.
- **A capped downside.** The boiler is not your problem.
- **Liquidity.** A down payment sitting in index funds is money you can reach.

## When buying does win

Time. Transaction costs are front-loaded, so the longer you stay, the more they amortise away. The rough rule is five years, longer in an expensive market.

## The real question

It is not "rent or buy". It is **how long will I be here, and what else would that down payment be doing?** Answer those honestly and the decision usually makes itself, and it is not always the one your relatives have opinions about.`,

  'wrong-number-scam': `## "Hi, is this Jessica?"

It is not. You reply to say so, because you are a normal person, and they are apologetic and warm. You end up chatting. They are interesting. This goes on for weeks.

At some point, never early, money comes up. They mention an investment platform. They are not pushy. They may even discourage you at first.

That reluctance is part of the script.

## What is actually happening

This is a long-form investment scam. The industry name for it is unpleasant and accurate: the victim is fattened up before the loss. It is run at scale, often by people who are themselves trafficked and forced to run it.

The whole thing is engineered:

- **The wrong number is not wrong.** It is sent to thousands of numbers, and the ones who reply have self-selected as people who reply.
- **The weeks of friendship are the product.** By the time money is mentioned, you are not evaluating a stranger's investment. You are considering a friend's.
- **The platform is a website.** The balance is a number someone types. Small withdrawals succeed early, which is the most persuasive thing that ever happens to you.

## The tells, in order of reliability

- **A relationship that began with a wrong number, wrong DM or wrong friend request.**
- **They will not video call**, or the one call is oddly short and blurry.
- **Returns that are steady.** Real markets are not steady. A chart that only goes up is a chart someone is drawing.
- **Withdrawals suddenly need a fee.** Tax, insurance, a release payment. There is never only one.
- **Urgency, late.** The window closes, the rate expires, the opportunity is ending.

## If you are in one

Stop paying. The fee to release your money does not release your money; it identifies you as someone who pays fees.

Then: screenshot everything, report it to the FTC at reportfraud.ftc.gov and to the FBI's IC3, and tell one real person. Shame is what keeps these running. The people who fall for this are not stupid, they are targeted for months by professionals, and staying quiet only protects the operation.`,

  'gift-card-tell': `## One rule, an entire family of scams

You can be talked into almost anything. Nobody can talk their way past this:

> **No legitimate organisation on earth will ever ask you to pay with a gift card.**

Not the IRS. Not your bank. Not the police. Not Apple, Amazon or Microsoft support. Not your electricity company. Not immigration. Not your grandson in jail.

Not once. Not ever.

## Why they want them specifically

A gift card is cash that has already left. Once you read the numbers off the back, the money is gone, it is untraceable, and it is unrecoverable. Wire transfers, crypto and payment apps sit in the same family for the same reason.

That is the entire appeal. It has nothing to do with the story attached.

## Which is why the story does not matter

Scripts change constantly: an unpaid tax bill, a warrant, a suspended account, a virus on your computer, a relative in trouble. Arguing with the story is the trap: it is designed to be argued with, and it is designed to frighten you into moving fast.

You do not need to evaluate it. Check the **payment method** instead. It is one question and it is never wrong.

## The three lines that end it

- "I do not pay anyone with gift cards."
- "I will call you back on the number printed on my card."
- Then hang up. You do not owe a scammer a polite exit.

## If it already happened

Move immediately, because occasionally the card has not been drained yet.

- Call the card issuer's fraud line, the number printed on the back of the card
- Keep the card and the receipt
- Report at reportfraud.ftc.gov

And tell someone. This one lands on careful, intelligent people all the time, because being careful is not the skill it defeats. Speed and fear are.`,
};

/** THE catalogue. Newest first, matching the synced list's own ordering contract. */
export const ALL_DIGEST_POSTS: ExtraPost[] = [...EXTRA_POSTS, ...(DIGEST_POSTS as ExtraPost[])];

export const ALL_DIGEST_CATEGORIES = [
  ...new Set(ALL_DIGEST_POSTS.map((p) => p.category)),
] as AnyDigestCategory[];

export const allBodyFor = (slug: string): string | undefined =>
  EXTRA_BODIES[slug] ?? DIGEST_BODIES[slug];
